# green — fix-sensor-late-tick-window

2026-10-18, HEAD `4bd0504` + this change's working tree. No compiled file is touched, so no Gradle run is
owed (guidelines/Build.md §2, scope block); the checks below are the sensor tooling's own offline suites, its
mutation harness and the structural checks of the files it edits.

## 1. The defect's own invocation, fixed

The late tick measures the rows it was scheduled for (the punchline of `red-on-head.log` §1):

```
$ bash tools/sensors/run.sh --due --dry-run --within 30 --now 2026-10-18T00:07:00Z --json
{"row":"broken-main","fingerprint":"83bb457d…","verdict":"clear","measurement":"success",…}
exit=0
```

The punctual tick is unchanged (`--now 2026-10-18T00:00:00Z`, with and without `--within 30`) and the
unwindowed late tick still prints `no rows selected` — that last one is asserted by
`run#aDelayedTickSelectsTheRowsItWasScheduledFor`, kept as the reason the window exists.

## 2. The suites

```
$ bash tools/sensor-selftest.sh
sensor-selftest: all 43 cases passed (JDK 27, offline, no token)        # was 3 of 43 red on HEAD

$ bash tools/sensor-mutations.sh
MUTATION 8.12: run#aDelayedTickSelectsTheRowsItWasScheduledFor red under the mutation, green after the restore — the window reaches back over the minutes since the previous tick
MUTATION 8.13: workflow#theTickDeclaresItsWindow red under the mutation, green after the restore — every scheduled tick declares the window it selects over
MUTATION 8.14: run#theWindowDoesNotSelectThePreviousTicksRow red under the mutation, green after the restore — a window that is not a count of minutes is refused
sensor-mutations: 14 behaved, 0 did not
```

